A||N||11||count_iblis||Saturn and the Fiero||||5||
Z||000000||count_iblis||11-17-2000||01:57 AM||||What does the Saturn have to do with the Fiero?  I had a friend today say "If it wasnt for the Fiero, Saturn would have never started.????????? I didnt get what he said so I just agreed with him.  Can someone please fill me in?  <P><BR>Chris <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/count-sig.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||stimpy||11-17-2000||03:24 AM||||Well, I don't know that Saturn would have never existed without the Fiero. I mean, the Fiero obviously exists, so kinda hard to disprove that theory. Back in the mid eighties, GM was looking to build import-fighters. Rather then try to notch them in under another makes reputation (which, in the eighties, wasn't terribly impressive) they elected to come up with a new marque. Enter Saturn.<BR>The Fiero was built (as we all know) using a groundbreaking construction technique of composite panels over a steel "safety cage" chassis. The benefits being very safe in crashes (a <B>major</B> Saturn selling point) and easily updated "sheetmetal" (for want of a better word)<BR>So, basically, Saturn co-opted the Fiero's constuction design, and, from what I understand, took a lot of the fabrication machinery when they opened their factory in Tennessee.  Obviously, this would decrease toolng costs for Saturn, making it much more viable to start a new marque. Plus, rather then being just another drably designed GM compact, it had <B>innovative design</B> and <B>dent resistant panels</B>, basically the same technology that everyone laughs at the Fiero for.  Heathens!<P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||11-17-2000||08:43 AM||||Yeah, what Stimpy said.<P>As part of the 1997 FOCOA National meet in Nashville, we toured the Saturn plant.  My wife (then girlfriend) only went to humor me - but once we were there, she was fascinated and said she wanted to get rid of her Honda and get a Saturn.  She now has a 1995 SL1 and loves it.  It has been a great car so far - 109K miles, runs/looks great.<P>I did get sad, though, watching the plant workers bolting polymer panels onto space frames and wishing they still made Fieros.||||reg||7
Z||000003||Oreif||11-17-2000||09:14 AM||||The reason we have Saturn is because we had the Fiero. This is because the Fiero Group at GM started the concept of the employees owning the plant. They were therefore in control of it. That is until GM found a loophole. The Fiero program was specified that they had to produce and sell 30,000 Fieros per year. By 1987 GM wanted to close down the Fiero program due to pressure from other divisions. Chevy thought it was the cause of the drop in sales of the corvette and Olds wanted a "clone" car. Also the "fire" rumors were getting abundant and they had a lot of recall work due to the large volume of cars sold in 1984. So for the 1988 model year GM cut the Fiero advertising budget by 70%. Since we all know the 88 model year had major brake and suspension improvements. They never were able to greatly advertise this. They still sold over 26,000 cars but GM said they didn't make 30,000 sales so they shut them down. Now the management that controlled the Fiero plant was offered the Saturn program. Since they were basically screwed by GM over the Fiero program, They negotiated the contract so that they had absolute power. They designed the car and They controlled all the money. The Saturn was suppose to come out in 1980 and be simular to the fiero in that they were to use "off the shelf" parts. Saturn said "No, We will design our own" That is also why Saturns do not use GM clone engines. The only thing Saturn took was the "mill and drill" machine for installing the plastic panels used to make the Fiero. (GM kept the other 2 for the Transport, Lumina, and Stilletto vans). A few years back GM stopped making the vans out of the plastic and sent the 2 other machines to Saturn. One is used for the new LS size cars and the third is used for the SUV that is comming out in 2001 (I think it is out now). So the Saturns are basically the offspring of the Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||11-17-2000||10:07 AM||||You know, Oreif, I never thought about this until now, but 1988 production was halted in March '88, right?  So that means '88 Fieros were only produced for a little over half a model year.  If production had continued through August like any other model year, they would have made around 45,000 1988 Fieros - just a hair under 1987 production.  Could the sales decline have been tapering off?<P>Stupid GM.  Mazda is making money on the Miata and they call it a huge success, and they haven't sold more than 25,000 Miatas in any one year since 1991.  Yet GM can't make money selling 45,000 Fieros a year?<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 11-17-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000005||Oreif||11-17-2000||10:22 AM||||In the early 90's Firebird and Camero sales were in the mid 20's for 2 or 3 years! They are still around. Actually in the Fiero Book by Gary W he talks about how corporate GM didn't like the Fiero from the beginning.<BR>But Pontiac pushed for it and and got it going. Imagine a Fiero today. Probably a 3800SC with an SLP HP boost!||||reg||1
Z||000006||fldevel||11-17-2000||10:33 AM||||Hmm .. why dont we all get together and protest at the GM main building to make a 2001 fiero.. andi would still like to learn the REAL reason why they scraped the fiero insted of all this hearsay.. none of it makes sence ||||reg||1
Z||000007||88t-top||11-17-2000||12:17 PM||||Just to answer a question a few post back, production was not stopped in March 88,just the decision to axe the Fiero.  Production kept going to the end of the model year.  My 88 t-top (#1004 of 1245 to have t-tops) was built in June 88 and Cars and Concepts received it 6-28-88.||||reg||1
Z||000008||KnightRyder31||11-17-2000||03:45 PM||||Some one told me that Fiero's had the same safty rating as the Saturns have today. But for some reason everyone insists on saying that the fiero is a death trap, know one says that about Saturns.<BR>I know first hand that Fiero's arent death traps. I was in the car with my brother when a Explorer ran over us(this was in HIS fiero). Niether of us were hurt at all, just the fiero(1984 Indy). The explorer was messed up also(the bumper was torn off, and the bottom of the truck was all over the road and the Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||11-17-2000||05:10 PM||||The Camaro and Firebirds may be bad examples to use.  The GM contract at the St. Therese plant in Canada expires in 2002.  It is looking like the F-bodies as we know them will be disappearing.  There are lots of rumors of their fate.  Some have one or the other being axed, while the remaining car becoming a traditional coupe (instead of a hatchback).  I have also seen rumors of the Camaro becoming strictly an upmarket V8 taking the place of the Vette so Chevy can put the Vette in a higher price level (read: Porsche, Viper).  Motor Trend recent article on the Opel Speedster indicates the roadster may replace the Firebird.  Basically, GM is being hush about it.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">  <BR>1988 Fiero Formula||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jaei Sun||11-17-2000||09:38 PM||||I thought you all might like reading this:<P>It backs up Fformula88 said about the Firebird.  Tho it might not happen this way.<P> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/sugarpixies/2002.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fformula88||11-17-2000||10:25 PM||||Be careful about getting excited about the Speedster showing up in the US.  It just doesn't sound like something GM would do.  The current factory output is 2000.  They have the ability to expand production to 4000.  Now, Motor Trend seems to think it will be imported, and C&D says no way.  There is also a tug-of-war between Pontiac and Saturn over who would actually get to sell it if they do import it.  I'd like to see it!  I am always in favor of having more sports cars on our roads (especially at the expense of SUV's).  However, if nearly 400,000 Fiero's over five years was not enough sales volume.... 2000 units a year certainly won't be.||||reg||1
